nt) if I discard all Characters. But I have no other Characters, so discarding W8 leaves me with only Bamboo and Dots, which is two suits, so \"One Voided Suit\" could apply if I win with that. That's only 1 point though.  \n\nNeed to think about scoring: I already have exposed melds, so no \"Concealed Hand\" etc. I need to accumulate points. Possibly \"All Chows\" (2 points) if my last meld is a chow and I have a pair of suited tiles. My pair T8 is suited. \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" not applicable. \"Dragon Pung\" none. \"Seat Wind\" pung? I have East Wind as seat wind and prevailing wind, but no F1 pung. \"Tile Hog\" if I have four identical, not. \"Mixed Double Pung\" not. \"Two Concealed Pungs\" no. \"Concealed Kong\" no. \"All Simples\" no because T1 terminal. So currently, I might only get \"All Chows\" (2) plus maybe \"Self Drawn\" (1) if I draw winning tile, or \"Edge Wait\"/\"Closed Wait\" etc. That's not 8 points. So I likely need to aim for a higher-point pattern. Given the game state, maybe I should just try to complete any hand and hope for lucky draws, but I must discard now.  \n\nConsidering opponents' discards: previous player played B2, T4, W4, etc. next player played J1, B1, etc. Perhaps Dots are relatively safe.  \n\nI'll choose to discard W8 to reduce suit clutter and keep pairs.  \n\nReason: Discarding W8 is safe (only one left unseen, less chance of giving pung) and it removes a stray tile from a third suit, simplifying my hand to two suits (Bamboo and Dots), which may help in forming chows or pungs within those suits. Keeping both pairs (T8 and B3) gives me two possible pairs for the final hand, and I can aim to draw a third copy of either to form a pung, or draw tiles to complete a chow. Among the singles, B2 and B7 have limited chow potential due to missing tiles (B4=0, B9=0), but B2 could pair with B3 if I draw B1 or B4? Actually B2,B3 need B1 or B4 for chow, but B4 is out, B1 is possible. B7 needs B6 or B8 for chow, B6 is plentiful (4